## Example

Let $E=\mathbb R^2$ with the dot product and standard vectors $e_1,e_2$. Set
$$
Y=\mathbb Z(e_1-e_2)+\mathbb Z(2e_2)=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z^2:a+b\text{ is even}\},\qquad X=\mathbb Z e_1+\mathbb Z\tfrac12(e_1+e_2).
$$
The displayed bases of $X$ and $Y$ are dual under the dot product. Let
$$
\Phi=\{\pm e_1,\pm e_2,\pm(e_1+e_2),\pm(e_1-e_2)\},
$$
with coroot $\alpha^\vee=2\alpha$ for the short roots $\alpha=\pm e_i$ and $\alpha^\vee=\alpha$ for the long roots $\alpha=\pm e_1\pm e_2$. Choose $\Delta=\{\alpha_1=e_1-e_2,\alpha_2=e_2\}$. The affine alcove is
$$
\mathcal A_0=\{(x_1,x_2):0<x_2<x_1,\ x_1+x_2<1\}.
$$
Its simple reflections are $s_1(x_1,x_2)=(x_2,x_1)$, $s_2(x_1,x_2)=(x_1,-x_2)$, and $s_0(x_1,x_2)=(1-x_2,1-x_1)$. The affine Iwahori–Matsumoto parameters are $q_{s_0}=q_{s_1}=2$ on the long-root walls and $q_{s_2}=3$ on the short-root wall.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The lattice pair $X,Y$, root and coroot sets, simple roots, and affine alcove stated above.

[F1] A reduced root datum consists of finite-rank free lattices in perfect integral duality, a finite root set and coroot set in bijection, and Weyl reflections preserving both sets ([[def-abstract-root-datum-and-its-weyl-group]]).

[F2] The Iwahori–Matsumoto algebra uses $(T_s-q_s)(T_s+1)=0$ and the affine Coxeter braid relations; $N_s=q_s^{-1/2}T_s$ satisfies $(N_s-q_s^{1/2})(N_s+q_s^{-1/2})=0$ ([[def-hh-affine-hecke-algebra-from-root-datum]]).

[F3] For a reduced crystallographic root system, affine reflections in the integer root hyperplanes generate the affine Coxeter group, whose simple reflections are those of the fundamental alcove; the product order of two simple wall reflections is determined by the angle between their walls ([[def-cg-affine-root-hyperplane-reflection-and-alcove]], [[thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length]]).

[F4] In the generic Coxeter Hecke presentation, parameters are allowed exactly when they are constant on the odd-edge connected components of the simple-generator graph; simple generators are conjugate exactly within those components ([[def-hh-universal-coxeter-hecke-parameters-and-presentation]]).

[L1] The elements $T_wU_\omega$ form a basis of the extended Iwahori–Matsumoto algebra ([[lem-hh-affine-hecke-im-basis]]).

## Verification

**Given:** The proposed $B_2$ root datum and affine Hecke parameters.

1.1 The vectors $e_1$ and $\tfrac12(e_1+e_2)$ form a basis of $X$, while $e_1-e_2$ and $2e_2$ form a basis of $Y$: an integer pair $(a,b)$ has even coordinate sum exactly when $(a,b)=a(e_1-e_2)+\frac{a+b}{2}(2e_2)$. The dot-product matrix between these two bases is the identity, so the pairing is perfect and integral. The roots lie in $X$, the coroots lie in $Y$, and direct pairings on $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ give the Cartan matrix $\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}$. The positive roots are $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_1+\alpha_2=e_1,\alpha_1+2\alpha_2=e_1+e_2$, so $\Delta$ is a base and the root set is reduced. The displayed root set is the union of the signed-permutation orbits of $\alpha_1$ and $\alpha_2$. The prescribed coroot map is equivariant under signed permutations: it doubles each short root and fixes each long-root vector, and signed permutations preserve root length. Thus the simple-root checks give $\langle\alpha,\alpha^\vee\rangle=2$ for every listed root, and the generated reflections preserve both root sets. The signed-permutation group generated by $s_1,s_2$ is finite. Therefore [F1] applies and these data are a reduced based $B_2$ root datum. [F1, given, algebra]

1.2 The simple walls of $\mathcal A_0$ are $x_1=x_2$, $x_2=0$, and $x_1+x_2=1$. In its interior, $0<x_2<1$, $0<x_1-x_2<1$, and $0<x_1+x_2<1$; the remaining positive-root function satisfies $0<x_1<1$ because $x_2>0$ and $x_1+x_2<1$. Thus no other integer root wall cuts it. Their reflections are respectively the displayed $s_1,s_2,s_0$. The product $s_1s_2$ is $(x_1,x_2)\mapsto(-x_2,x_1)$, a quarter-turn of order $4$. The product $s_2s_0$ is $(x_1,x_2)\mapsto(1-x_2,x_1-1)$; it fixes $(1,0)$ and its linear part about that point is the same quarter-turn, so it has order $4$. The product $s_0s_1$ is $(x_1,x_2)\mapsto(1-x_1,1-x_2)$, a half-turn of order $2$. Hence the affine Coxeter labels are $m(s_1,s_2)=m(s_2,s_0)=4$ and $m(s_1,s_0)=2$. [F3, given, algebra]

2.1 The affine roots defining $s_0$ and $s_1$ are long and the one defining $s_2$ is short. The displayed basis of $Y$ is $\alpha_1^\vee,\alpha_2^\vee$, so $Y=Q^\vee$ and $\Omega=1$ by [F2]. Thus conjugacy in the extended group is conjugacy in the affine Coxeter group. The Coxeter labels just computed are all even, so the odd-edge graph has three singleton components; by [F4] the three assigned positive parameters are constant on every required conjugacy class. In the symmetric convention the parameters are $v_{s_0}=v_{s_1}=\sqrt2$ and $v_{s_2}=\sqrt3$, with the quadratic relations of [F2]. [F2, F4, step 1.2, algebra]

3.1 Here $Y=Q^\vee$, so the extended group has trivial length-zero quotient and $\Omega=\{1\}$. Applying [L1] to the affine Coxeter system identified in step 1.2, with the valid affine Coxeter parameters from step 2.1, shows that $\{T_w:w\in W_{\mathrm{aff}}\}$ is a $\mathbb C$-basis. This verifies the claimed unequal-parameter affine $B_2$ example. [F2, L1, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1] ∎

